WEBVTT
NOTE does-damascus-steel-rust - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.531
Leave a prized patterned blade wet
overnight, and by morning it can bloom

00:00:05.531 --> 00:00:10.461
Yes: most Damascus steel rusts, because
nearly all of it, historical crucible

00:00:10.461 --> 00:00:15.391
blades and modern pattern-welded knives
alike, is carbon steel without enough

00:00:15.391 --> 00:00:17.120
chromium to protect itself.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.528
ASM International, the professional
society for materials engineers, gives the

00:00:22.528 --> 00:00:28.005
working line: steel needs at least 10.5
percent chromium to count as stainless.

00:00:28.005 --> 00:00:33.109
Most Damascus sits far below that. But
that number settles less than it seems to.

00:00:33.109 --> 00:00:38.495
It does not explain why one patterned
blade stays bright for years while another

00:00:38.495 --> 00:00:39.707
spots within days.

00:00:39.707 --> 00:00:43.978
It does not explain why the pattern itself
can make the problem worse.

00:00:43.978 --> 00:00:47.395
For that, you need to know what the
pattern actually is.

00:00:47.395 --> 00:00:51.101
The first thing to settle is that the word
does not describe one material.

00:00:51.101 --> 00:00:56.475
Historically, Damascus referred to blades
forged from crucible steel, a high-carbon

00:00:56.475 --> 00:01:01.266
steel made by melting iron with
carbon-bearing material in a sealed vessel

00:01:01.266 --> 00:01:02.691
and cooling it slowly.

00:01:02.691 --> 00:01:08.003
That steel was produced in South Asia and
traded west, and smiths forged it into

00:01:08.003 --> 00:01:11.656
swords whose surfaces showed a flowing,
watery pattern.

00:01:11.656 --> 00:01:17.127
Today, most knives sold as Damascus are
something else: pattern-welded steel.

00:01:17.127 --> 00:01:22.811
A smith stacks layers of two different
steels, heats them, hammers them into one

00:01:22.811 --> 00:01:25.795
bar, folds or twists the bar, and repeats.

00:01:25.795 --> 00:01:30.911
So when you ask whether Damascus rusts,
you are really asking about two families

00:01:30.911 --> 00:01:32.957
of steel, not one single recipe.

00:01:32.957 --> 00:01:38.264
The two families share one important
trait. Neither recipe is stainless by

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:43.231
Historical crucible steel was iron and
carbon with small amounts of other

00:01:43.231 --> 00:01:45.408
elements picked up from the ore.

00:01:45.408 --> 00:01:50.168
Its chromium content was nowhere near the
threshold that defines stainless.

00:01:50.168 --> 00:01:55.119
Modern pattern-welded billets are usually
built from a plain high-carbon steel

00:01:55.119 --> 00:02:00.260
paired with a nickel-bearing steel, chosen
because the two respond differently to

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.608
acid and give strong visual contrast.

00:02:02.608 --> 00:02:07.019
Nickel helps a little with corrosion, but
it is not what makes steel stainless.

00:02:07.019 --> 00:02:07.885
Chromium is.

00:02:07.885 --> 00:02:13.733
And in most Damascus, chromium is either
absent or present only in trace amounts.

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:18.069
To see why that matters, it helps to be
precise about what rust is.

00:02:18.069 --> 00:02:23.472
Rust is not dirt and it is not simple
decay. It is an electrochemical reaction.

00:02:23.472 --> 00:02:28.176
When iron, oxygen and water meet, tiny
regions of the metal surface begin to

00:02:28.176 --> 00:02:30.528
behave like the two ends of a battery.

00:02:30.528 --> 00:02:34.501
At one region, iron atoms give up
electrons and go into solution.

00:02:34.501 --> 00:02:38.740
At another, oxygen dissolved in the water
takes those electrons.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:44.170
The products combine into hydrated iron
oxide, the flaky orange-brown material you

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:44.832
recognise.

00:02:44.832 --> 00:02:48.987
Take away the water or take away the
oxygen, and the reaction stalls.

00:02:48.987 --> 00:02:54.453
That is why a dry blade in a dry room can
sit unchanged for a very long time.

00:02:54.453 --> 00:02:57.396
Salt changes the speed, not the basic
chemistry.

00:02:57.396 --> 00:03:02.299
Dissolved salt makes water a much better
conductor, so the little battery on the

00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:03.525
surface runs faster.

00:03:03.525 --> 00:03:06.611
Chloride ions also attack protective films
directly.

00:03:06.611 --> 00:03:11.300
This is why a blade used to cut salted
food, or carried near the sea, or simply

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:16.107
handled with sweaty fingers, can rust far
faster than one wiped with plain water.

00:03:16.107 --> 00:03:18.966
Salt is an electrolyte that speeds the
current.

00:03:18.966 --> 00:03:21.339
It is not the cause of rust on its own.

00:03:21.339 --> 00:03:27.466
The cause is unprotected iron in contact
with oxygen and moisture, and carbon steel

00:03:27.466 --> 00:03:29.312
Damascus is exactly that.

00:03:29.312 --> 00:03:31.462
So what does chromium actually do?

00:03:31.462 --> 00:03:36.395
It does not make steel harder in any way
that stops rust, and that distinction

00:03:36.395 --> 00:03:36.901
matters.

00:03:36.901 --> 00:03:40.510
Hardness and corrosion resistance are
separate properties.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:43.808
A blade can be extremely hard and still
rust quickly.

00:03:43.808 --> 00:03:49.634
What chromium does is react with oxygen to
form a thin, self-repairing chromium oxide

00:03:49.634 --> 00:03:53.061
layer on the surface, often called a
passive film.

00:03:53.061 --> 00:03:58.062
That film is far too thin to see, but it
is continuous and tightly bonded, and it

00:03:58.062 --> 00:04:01.643
blocks water and oxygen from reaching the
iron underneath.

00:04:01.643 --> 00:04:06.862
If you scratch it, the exposed chromium
reacts with the air and the film closes

00:04:06.862 --> 00:04:07.259
again.

00:04:07.259 --> 00:04:12.456
As ASM International describes it, the
film forms continuously only above the

00:04:12.456 --> 00:04:13.739
chromium threshold.

00:04:13.739 --> 00:04:18.435
The threshold of 10.5 percent exists
because below roughly that level, there is

00:04:18.435 --> 00:04:23.013
not enough chromium at the surface for the
film to form as an unbroken sheet.

00:04:23.013 --> 00:04:27.861
You get patches of protection with gaps
between them, and corrosion starts in the

00:04:27.861 --> 00:04:28.160
gaps.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.955
That is the real reason most Damascus
rusts.

00:04:30.955 --> 00:04:34.576
It is not that the steel is old-fashioned
or poorly made.

00:04:34.576 --> 00:04:38.773
It is that the alloy was never designed to
carry a passive film at all.

00:04:38.773 --> 00:04:43.596
It was designed to hold a keen edge and to
show a pattern, and those goals pull its

00:04:43.596 --> 00:04:45.456
composition away from stainless.

00:04:45.456 --> 00:04:49.257
Most big engineering disasters look like
bad luck in the moment.

00:04:49.257 --> 00:04:51.989
Go backward and you find a chain of
decisions.

00:04:51.989 --> 00:04:55.391
That observation applies on a small scale
to a single knife.

00:04:55.391 --> 00:04:59.812
The rust you see is the end of a chain,
and one link is the way the pattern is

00:04:59.812 --> 00:05:00.549
made visible.

00:05:00.549 --> 00:05:04.944
Smiths forge layers of two steels
together, and after forging, a

00:05:04.944 --> 00:05:07.211
pattern-welded blade looks plain.

00:05:07.211 --> 00:05:13.105
The smith must grind the blade flat and
then etch in acid, commonly a ferric

00:05:13.105 --> 00:05:14.501
chloride solution.

00:05:14.501 --> 00:05:18.842
The acid attacks the two steels at
different rates, so the layers dissolve at

00:05:18.842 --> 00:05:19.744
different rates.

00:05:19.744 --> 00:05:22.781
The high-carbon layers darken and recede
slightly.

00:05:22.781 --> 00:05:26.608
The nickel-bearing layers stay bright and
stand a little proud.

00:05:26.608 --> 00:05:31.459
The pattern you admire is, quite
literally, a map of where the acid found

00:05:31.459 --> 00:05:33.387
the metal easier to dissolve.

00:05:33.387 --> 00:05:35.777
Think about what that leaves behind.

00:05:35.777 --> 00:05:41.087
An etched surface is a fine landscape of
ridges and valleys, not a smooth sealed

00:05:41.087 --> 00:05:41.552
finish.

00:05:41.552 --> 00:05:45.493
The valleys can hold moisture and salt
after you rinse the blade.

00:05:45.493 --> 00:05:50.590
And the surface now presents two different
metals side by side, both wetted by the

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:51.771
same film of water.

00:05:51.771 --> 00:05:56.741
When two dissimilar metals sit in contact
through an electrolyte, the less noble one

00:05:56.741 --> 00:05:58.635
tends to corrode preferentially.

00:05:58.635 --> 00:06:03.107
This is ordinary galvanic corrosion, the
same principle engineers manage when they

00:06:03.107 --> 00:06:05.125
join different metals in a structure.

00:06:05.125 --> 00:06:09.829
How strong the effect is on a given blade
depends on the specific steels and the

00:06:09.829 --> 00:06:12.416
finish, and that varies from maker to
maker.

00:06:12.416 --> 00:06:16.098
How is any of this actually known, rather
than assumed?

00:06:16.098 --> 00:06:19.579
Corrosion resistance is measured, not
judged by eye.

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:25.315
ASTM International publishes the standard
salt spray test, designated B117, in which

00:06:25.315 --> 00:06:30.709
samples sit in a chamber filled with a
fine fog of salt water and are inspected

00:06:30.709 --> 00:06:31.392
over time.

00:06:31.392 --> 00:06:35.256
NIST, the United States national
measurement laboratory, carries out

00:06:35.256 --> 00:06:38.437
corrosion measurement research on how
materials degrade.

00:06:38.437 --> 00:06:44.288
Tests like these let a lab compare alloys
under identical, repeatable conditions, so

00:06:44.288 --> 00:06:49.303
a claim that one steel outlasts another
rests on observation rather than

00:06:49.303 --> 00:06:50.069
reputation.

00:06:50.069 --> 00:06:54.853
But you should be clear about what such a
test does and does not settle.

00:06:54.853 --> 00:06:59.351
It is good at comparing alloys under
identical conditions, and it gives

00:06:59.351 --> 00:07:00.555
repeatable results.

00:07:00.555 --> 00:07:04.155
It is not good at predicting service life
in a real kitchen.

00:07:04.155 --> 00:07:09.663
The ASTM standard itself cautions that
salt spray results do not reliably predict

00:07:09.663 --> 00:07:13.131
how a material will behave in natural
environments.

00:07:13.131 --> 00:07:17.991
A knife on a counter faces cycles of
wetting, drying, food acids and handling

00:07:17.991 --> 00:07:20.389
that no single fog chamber reproduces.

00:07:20.389 --> 00:07:26.295
So lab data can rank steels, while daily
use decides how fast a particular blade

00:07:26.295 --> 00:07:27.403
actually rusts.

00:07:27.403 --> 00:07:30.654
The historical blades add a different kind
of evidence.

00:07:30.654 --> 00:07:35.266
In 2006, a team led by Marianne Reibold at
the Technical University of Dresden

00:07:35.266 --> 00:07:39.878
reported in the journal Nature that they
had examined a centuries-old Damascus

00:07:39.878 --> 00:07:42.597
sabre with a transmission electron
microscope.

00:07:42.597 --> 00:07:48.049
After dissolving a sample in acid, they
found carbon nanotubes and what appeared

00:07:48.049 --> 00:07:53.500
to be nanowires of cementite, the hard
iron carbide that forms bands in crucible

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:53.909
steel.

00:07:53.909 --> 00:07:58.788
The finding drew attention because it
suggested that old smithing, done without

00:07:58.788 --> 00:08:03.728
any knowledge of microstructure, produced
remarkably fine internal architecture.

00:08:03.728 --> 00:08:06.657
It is worth noticing what that study did
not show.

00:08:06.657 --> 00:08:11.168
It was evidence about internal structure,
not evidence about rust resistance.

00:08:11.168 --> 00:08:16.214
Fine carbide bands and nanoscale features
help explain the toughness and the visible

00:08:16.214 --> 00:08:17.776
pattern of crucible steel.

00:08:17.776 --> 00:08:20.232
They do nothing to create a passive film.

00:08:20.232 --> 00:08:25.083
Many surviving historical blades are in
good condition, but museum survival tells

00:08:25.083 --> 00:08:29.874
you about storage, oiling and handling
over centuries, not about the steel being

00:08:29.874 --> 00:08:30.293
immune.

00:08:30.293 --> 00:08:35.268
Blades that were neglected did not survive
to be studied, which quietly biases what

00:08:35.268 --> 00:08:38.384
you see in a display case toward the
well-cared-for.

00:08:38.384 --> 00:08:42.880
There is a third kind of Damascus, and it
answers the question differently.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:47.904
Some makers forge pattern-welded billets
from two stainless alloys, each above the

00:08:47.904 --> 00:08:50.661
stainless threshold of 10.5 percent
chromium.

00:08:50.661 --> 00:08:55.215
The finished blade carries a passive film
across its whole surface, so it resists

00:08:55.215 --> 00:08:57.632
rust far better than carbon steel
Damascus.

00:08:57.632 --> 00:09:03.004
Plain carbon steel, by contrast, is made
with essentially no deliberate chromium,

00:09:03.004 --> 00:09:04.264
close to 0 percent.

00:09:04.264 --> 00:09:08.835
The trade-off is usually visual: stainless
alloys are chemically similar to each

00:09:08.835 --> 00:09:13.235
other, so the acid has less difference to
work with, and the pattern can look

00:09:13.235 --> 00:09:13.635
softer.

00:09:13.635 --> 00:09:16.561
Even here, stainless does not mean
stain-proof.

00:09:16.561 --> 00:09:21.666
The passive film can break down locally
when chloride sits on it, producing small,

00:09:21.666 --> 00:09:23.720
deep pits rather than broad rust.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:28.715
A stainless Damascus blade left in salty
water, or stored damp in a sheath, can

00:09:28.715 --> 00:09:29.411
still mark.

00:09:29.411 --> 00:09:34.323
The resistance is real and large, but it
is resistance, not immunity.

00:09:34.323 --> 00:09:39.161
So the honest answer for this third family
is that it rarely rusts in normal use, and

00:09:39.161 --> 00:09:43.032
it can still corrode if conditions are
harsh enough for long enough.

00:09:43.032 --> 00:09:47.231
For carbon steel Damascus, then, the
protection comes from you.

00:09:47.231 --> 00:09:52.563
Dry it promptly after use, so water never
has time to sit in the etched valleys.

00:09:52.563 --> 00:09:57.741
Keep a thin film of oil on it, which keeps
oxygen and moisture away from the metal.

00:09:57.741 --> 00:09:59.347
Avoid leaving salt on it.

00:09:59.347 --> 00:10:04.165
Many owners also let a dark patina
develop, a stable oxide layer that slows

00:10:04.165 --> 00:10:07.699
further attack, and some smiths apply one
deliberately.

00:10:07.699 --> 00:10:10.632
What never protects the blade is the
pattern itself.

00:10:10.632 --> 00:10:15.370
The pattern is the record of how the steel
was made, and the chemistry underneath it

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:16.611
remains ordinary iron.

00:10:16.611 --> 00:10:20.930
When you watch this back, notice where
each claim comes from.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:25.533
The chromium threshold is a definition
used by ASM International.

00:10:25.533 --> 00:10:30.566
The salt spray method is a published ASTM
International standard, with its own

00:10:30.566 --> 00:10:31.469
stated limits.

00:10:31.469 --> 00:10:35.773
The nanotube finding comes from one study
in Nature, on one sabre.

00:10:35.773 --> 00:10:39.876
The rest is the ordinary electrochemistry
of iron, oxygen and water.

00:10:39.876 --> 00:10:44.402
The chain runs from composition, to
etching, to moisture and salt, to rust,

00:10:44.402 --> 00:10:49.048
and each link is a separate piece of
evidence rather than one sweeping claim.

00:10:49.048 --> 00:10:53.779
What this does not establish is how
quickly any particular Damascus knife will

00:10:53.779 --> 00:10:58.570
The threshold of 10.5 percent tells you
whether a continuous film can form; it

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:03.238
says nothing precise about a blade below
it, where protection is partial and

00:11:03.238 --> 00:11:08.397
depends on the exact alloys, the depth of
the etch, the finish and the care it gets.

00:11:08.397 --> 00:11:12.984
Makers rarely publish full compositions
for their billets, and lab tests do not

00:11:12.984 --> 00:11:14.493
predict life in a kitchen.

00:11:14.493 --> 00:11:18.936
Nor does one sabre in one study speak for
every historical blade.

00:11:18.936 --> 00:11:23.997
Confidence in the threshold is high.
Confidence about any single blade is low.

00:11:23.997 --> 00:11:29.920
So yes, most Damascus steel rusts, because
the beauty of the pattern comes from the

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:32.632
same unprotected iron that rust needs.

00:11:32.632 --> 00:11:35.821
The swirl is a record of fire, hammer and
acid.

00:11:35.821 --> 00:11:39.552
Keeping it bright is still up to the cloth
and the oil.
